#
# Transitive isFedBy: everything whose output eventually reaches this asset.
#
# PARAMETER: ?asset -- bound by the caller to an equipment node.
#
# This is the root cause direction. Given a symptom observed here, these are the
# only assets that could physically have caused it by way of what they deliver.
# Anything not in this result is not a candidate, which is what stops the
# diagnosis layer from blaming an unrelated machine that happens to be degrading
# at the same time.
#
# ^brick:feeds is brick:isFedBy. Brick declares the two as formal inverses --
# confirmed by reading the published ontology, which states
# brick:isFedBy owl:inverseOf brick:feeds -- but we do not load the Brick
# ontology and rdflib performs no OWL reasoning, so asking for brick:isFedBy by
# name returns nothing at all. Writing the inverse as a property path is the same
# statement semantically and needs no reasoner. The + makes it transitive.
#
# No hop count: SPARQL property paths report reachability, not path length.
# model/graph.py annotates each row by walking the same edges breadth-first, and
# cross-checks that the two agree on which nodes are reachable.
#

PREFIX brick: <https://brickschema.org/schema/Brick#>

SELECT DISTINCT ?upstream ?upstream_class
WHERE {
    ?asset (^brick:feeds)+ ?upstream .
    OPTIONAL { ?upstream a ?upstream_class }
}
